[A newspaper article, posted with the author's permission. -cmk]

===========================================
 
Independent Commentary 11/11/93
 
The Harvard Independent -- Commentary Policy:  The opinions
expressed in the Commentary section are the authors' own and
do not necessarily reflect the views of the Independent's
editors.  The Independent welcomes commentaries and responses
from all Harvard students and  faculty members, on a range of
national and campus issues.
 
"Harvard sponsors cyberporn"
 
The Internet plays host to violent sex stories, and you pay
the bill.
 
by David Sherwood
 
So I'm sitting in front of my computer, and this is what I
read:      
 
"WARNING:  contains non-consensual sex *between* minors, this
particular story is m/m/f, and pretty damned violent,
depending on your perspective of course, as there's no actual
dismemberment or mutilation it may not be quite violent
enough(?)  This is fiction.  I didn't do these things, I don't
think YOU should.  But it's not a crime to whack off to the
fantasy, at least not until the government can figure out how
to monitor brain-waves.  I'm not responsible for your actions
and you're  not responsible for mine."
 
After this header, I read a story about two high-school boys
who kidnap a 12-year-old Asian girl and proceed to beat and
gang rape her in every conceivable way -- all the while
claiming that women are "dirty sluts" who "love it".
 
A bit disturbing?  Well, it's even more disturbing,
considering that *you* paid for it.
 
That's right, folks:  Your tuition money, besides paying for
bloated UC budgets and the yummy food at the Union, is being
used to provide us with stories about the "deflowering" of a
six-year-old girl, the kidnap and rape of a group of Cub
Scouts, and the transformation of a married woman into the
"bitch" of a large dog.
 
Harvard, as an official gateway of the Internet computer
network, subscribes to some 2,000 newsgroups, among them
alt.sex.stories, which  carried these sordid pieces.  Harvard
can pull the plug on any newsgroup it chooses to without
losing access to the network's other benefits.      Only an
institution with the clout and power of Harvard can create 
the consensus needed to enforce some sort of decency on the
Internet.  If  we can restrict what's published in magazines,
surely we can do the same for electronic publications.
 
So I don't think the Internet needs to be censored *per se*,
only that Harvard should let me choose whether I wish to help
pay for the electronic distribution of a story about the rape
and murder of an 8-year-old girl.
 
The stories I've listed are from just *one* Internet news
group; keep in mind that there are dozens of similar groups,
dealing with every conceivable aspect of pornography --
including bondage, bestiality, pedophilia, and dismemberment.
 
But the danger of these stories is not so much in their
subjects as in the realism they convey.  Sexual violence in
cyberspace is far more dangerous than in smut magazines or
porno movies.  On a comupter network,  most of the postings
of new material are anonymous.  These are graphic, explicit
descriptions of people's darkest fantasies.  Without the
glossy pictures or ridiculous acting of mainstream
pornography, Internet porn provides a haven for outlawed forms
of sexual deviance.      
 
You might say, "So what?  If you don't like it, then don't
read it." That would be OK if you were talking to adults --
but there are other people on the Internet besides geeky
computer science majors.
 
Last year, I helped teach in a computer lab in a New York City
elementary school.  Every student had full access to the
Internet from the age of eight. This means that the 10-year-
old boy I tutored, who is just now beginning to look at girls,
might stumble upon alt.sex.stories (or be tipped off by a
friend).  He might begin to think that raping his sister is
something that goes on all the time, because all girls are
"dirty sluts" who "want it."      
 
We all have the right to say and think whatever we wish, but
if showing violent sexual perversions to 13-year-old boys
isn't shouting "fire" in a crowded theater, then I don't know
what is.  So the next time you're searching for the
explanation for some horrid sex crime and are looking to point
the finger at some "blameworthy" institution, look no further
than Harvard.  And then remember who pays for it.
 
-End
