lusardi@cs.buffalo.edu (Christopher Lusardi) writes:

>At my university, all our postings are sent to a monitor. He has told me
>that UB posts about 300 articles per day. He scans them for multiple
>postings, et cetera. If he thinks something shouldn't be posted he sends
>email to higher ups and the article or articles are canceled et cetera.

Email from the sys admin there clarifies that they are not doing prior
restraint. Here is what they are doing: All local postings are send
out *and* send to a mailing list. Articles in the mailing list are
reviewed by a university computer staff member.  If, in the opinion of
the staff member, an articles violate computer policy or copyright
law, he or she notifies the author. He or she will sometimes also
issue a cancel of the article.

In my non-lawyer opinion, this means that the U. at Buffalo is
spending computer staff resources to *increase* the University's
liability. I strongly suggest they talk to their university legal
counsel before continuing. Have university legal counsel call EFF
legal services (finger godwin@eff.org) for information about the case
law.

Also, does the University pay for methodical surveillance of other
university forums? For example, does the University pay an employee to
listen to student speakers on the Green/Quad. Does it pay an employee
to collect and review all paper publications? Does it authorize that
employee to try to destroy the publications he or she judges (on his
or her own) violate policy or law?

Computer forums should be treated like other forums. If surveillance
and summary destruction of expressive material is not allowed in other
forums, it should not occur in computer forums.

- Carl
